CMOS VLSI 
***********************
CMOS VLSI (Complementary Metal-Oxide-Semiconductor Very-Large-Scale Integration) is the dominant technology used to create modern integrated circuits (ICs), allowing millions or billions of transistors to be packed onto a single silicon chip. 
Core Concepts
CMOS Technology: Uses complementary pairs of p-type (PMOS) and n-type (NMOS) transistors.
VLSI Scale: Refers to the integration of thousands to billions of transistors into one chip, enabling complex systems like microprocessors and memory.
Low Power Consumption: CMOS circuits draw significant power only during switching, leading to very low static power dissipation and minimal waste heat compared to older technologies. 
Design & Architecture
Logic Structure: CMOS gates consist of a Pull-Up Network (PUN) of PMOS transistors and a Pull-Down Network (PDN) of NMOS transistors.
The CMOS Inverter: The most fundamental building block. When the input is low, the PMOS conducts (output high); when the input is high, the NMOS conducts (output low).
Physical Design: Involves creating a layout on silicon using processes like n-well, p-well, or twin-tub to manage electrical properties and prevent issues like latch-up. 

